    "content": "The capital of Ethiopia's northern Tigray region, Mekelle, continues to operate without a fully functioning civilian administration, several days after forces aligned with the Ethiopian federal government took control of the city. According to reports, government offices, police stations, and the mayor's office remained closed on Thursday, and no interim body has been announced to oversee administrative duties and public services.\n\nThe closure of these institutions indicates that the takeover has not yet resulted in the restoration of civilian governance or basic services. It remains unclear when or how such services will resume, as no transitional authority has been publicly named.\n\nThis development follows the entry of federal-aligned forces into Mekelle, though the exact timeline of the takeover and the circumstances leading to it have not been fully detailed. The situation remains fluid, with local residents facing uncertainty about the provision of essential services.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom have family and business ties in Ethiopia and closely follow developments in the Horn of Africa, the lack of a functioning administration in Mekelle raises concerns about stability and humanitarian access in the region. The absence of clear authority could exacerbate existing challenges, including disruptions to trade routes and remittance flows that affect diaspora communities.\n\nObservers note that the vacuum in civilian governance may hinder the delivery of aid and complicate efforts to normalize daily life in Mekelle. The international community has previously urged all parties to ensure the protection of civilians and the restoration of essential services.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/09/mekelle-oo-weli-aan-lahayn-maamul-rayid-ah-oo-shaqeynaya-kaddib-la-wareegiddii-ciidamada-dowladda-itoobiya/",
