    "id": "news-1791483092856-5",
    "title": "Somali Cabinet Approves 9-Member Board for National Fisheries Company",
    "original_title": "Golaha Wasiirrada oo Ansixiyay Guddiga Maamulka Shirkadda Kalluumaysiga Qaranka",
    "slug": "somali-cabinet-approves-9-member-board-for-national-fisheries-company",
    "summary": "The Somali Federal Government's Council of Ministers approved a nine-member management board for the Somali National Fisheries Company, following a proposal from the Ministry of Fisheries and Blue Economy. The move advances the implementation of the 2025 law establishing the company.",
    "content": "The Council of Ministers of the Federal Government of Somalia has approved a nine-member management board for the Somali National Fisheries Company. The decision was made during a cabinet session chaired by Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre, following a proposal from the Ministry of Fisheries and Blue Economy. The board members were endorsed through a show of hands, according to a statement released after the meeting.\n\nThe approval is part of the ongoing implementation of a cabinet decision from October 30, 2025, which established the legal framework for the National Fisheries Company. That earlier decision laid the groundwork for the company's creation, and today's move fills a key governance gap by appointing its leadership. The Ministry of Fisheries and Blue Economy, led by Minister Ahmed Hassan Adan, has been tasked with overseeing the sector's development.\n\nThe new board is expected to guide the company's operations, which aim to manage and develop Somalia's marine resources. Somalia has one of the longest coastlines in Africa, stretching over 3,300 kilometers, yet the fisheries sector remains underdeveloped due to decades of conflict, illegal fishing, and limited investment. The establishment of a national fisheries company is seen as a step toward asserting control over these resources and generating revenue for the federal government.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development carries particular significance. Many diaspora members have ties to coastal communities and have long called for better stewardship of Somalia's maritime assets. A functioning national fisheries company could create economic opportunities, including joint ventures, employment, and investment channels that diaspora entrepreneurs could tap into. It also signals progress in rebuilding state institutions, a key concern for those who support Somalia's stabilization from abroad.\n\nThe approval of the board is the latest in a series of measures by the federal government to strengthen the blue economy. In recent years, Somalia has sought to combat illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing through international partnerships and legal reforms. The new board will be responsible for translating these policies into tangible outcomes, though challenges such as security, infrastructure, and regulatory enforcement remain.\n\nObservers note that the success of the fisheries company will depend on the board's ability to operate transparently and effectively. The diaspora, which remits billions of dollars annually to Somalia, could play a role in supporting the sector through investment and expertise if the enabling environment improves. The government has not yet announced when the board will formally assume its duties.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/08/golaha-wasiirrada-oo-ansixiyay-guddiga-maamulka-shirkadda-kalluumaysiga-qaranka/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
    "source_name": "Goobjoog",
    "original_url": "https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/08/golaha-wasiirrada-oo-ansixiyay-guddiga-maamulka-shirkadda-kalluumaysiga-qaranka/",
    "country": "Somalia",
